course: dictionary
lesson: 9998
title: One word, more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Look at the words around the tricky word, then try each meaning in the sentence to see which one fits.
explain: «Before the telegraph, a long message could travel only as fast as a person, horse or ship could carry it.» Here telegraph is the old invention that sent messages along wires, so it is a noun, the name of a thing.
explain: «He telegraphed his pass, so the other team saw it coming.» Here telegraph is a verb. It means showing clearly, without meaning to, what you are going to do. Try the first meaning here and it does not fit, so the sentence has chosen the second.
fact: A telegraph was an old way of sending messages along wires, using electric signals such as Morse code.
fact: Telegraph can be a noun (a telegraph) or a verb (to telegraph a message, which means to send it by telegraph).
fact: A telegraph pole is a tall wooden pole that holds up phone or electricity wires.
fact: From the 1840s, messages could be sent by telegraph as coded clicks along wires, before telephones existed.
fact: Informal use: to telegraph something means to show clearly what you are going to do, often by accident.
exercise: «Before the telegraph, a long message could travel only as fast as a person, horse or ship could carry it.» What is the telegraph in this sentence? | A horse that carries letters | A tall wooden pole for holding up wires | *An old way of sending messages along wires | To show what you are going to do
exercise: «He telegraphed his pass, so the other team saw it coming.» What does telegraphed mean here? | Kicked the ball very hard | *Showed clearly what he was going to do | Ran off the pitch | Sent a message by wire
exercise: «The telegraph pole stood beside the road.» What does a telegraph pole hold up? | Bridges | *Phone and electricity wires | Flags | Tents
test: «News of Lincoln's death in 1865 took about 12 days to reach Europe.» Why did the news take so long? | Lincoln did not send the message | *Messages could only travel as fast as a ship or person, and there was no telegraph cable across the Atlantic yet | The ships had no sails | Europe had no newspapers
test: In «They will telegraph the news to the office by morning», what kind of word is telegraph? | Adverb | Noun | *Verb | Adjective
exam: «The clerk telegraphed the message to the next town.» What does telegraphed mean in this sentence? | *Sent the message by telegraph | Showed clearly what the clerk was going to do | Wrote the message on a tall wooden pole | Carried the message on a horse
